@node install "Installation"
@toc main
@next flight

  Copy Space.000 and Space.020 to an Amiga drawer. Run from Shell:

  Space.000 --ecs
  Space.020 --aga
  Space.020 --rtg

  Native modes require Kickstart 3.0 or later and detect PAL/NTSC.
  Add --lowres on PAL for a centred 320 x 200 display.
  AGA requires an AGA chipset. RTG needs CyberGraphX V41 or a
  Picasso96 setup with the compatible CyberGraphX API.
  Expansion RAM is needed; stock A500 performance is not established.

@endnode

@node flight "Flying Your Ship"
@toc main
@prev install
@next navigation

  L         Launch from a port or open ground.
  Arrows    Pitch and yaw.
  RMouse    Hold and drag as a virtual stick; release to recenter.
  LMouse    Fire laser or turret.
  Joystick  Port 2 directions steer; fire button shoots.
  W / S     Increase or decrease set speed.
  E         Toggle flight assist / direct thrust.
  Ctrl      Fire forward laser, or the aimed turret in turret views.
  K         Raise/lower landing gear while flying.
  P         Toggle autopilot to the selected port.
  T         Cycle port targets.
  Q         Cycle planet/moon approach waypoints.
  D         Request manual docking clearance.
  V         Return to flight; cycle front/rear/external/dorsal/ventral.
  R         Cycle contacts within the 10 km radar sphere.
  Z / I     Open orbital map / system and body data.
  Space     Pause/resume simulation.
  H         Show on-screen help.
  Escape    Exit and restore Workbench.

  Mouse: drag right/left for yaw, up/down for pitch, with a small dead zone.
  Use the mouse port and a digital joystick in port 2. On the host preview,
  axes 0/1 or a direction pad steer, button 0 fires. W/S controls thrust.
  Autopilot retains steering until you disengage it with P.

  In turret views arrows, mouse and joystick aim independently of the hull. W/S still controls
  ship thrust; Ctrl fires along turret aim. Pitch stops short of the poles.
  Radar follows hull heading, forward at top; stems show vertical distance.
  Red contacts are hostile, green friendly, gold selected. Selected contacts
  show a diamond, hull, range and signed closing speed. Navigation targets
  stay independent. Turret aim and contact selection reset on loading.

  Start with L, then P, to fly from Haven to its orbital Relay.
  Space preserves momentum. Turning does not instantly change velocity.
  With assist disabled, W/S applies forward/reverse thrust directly.
  Manual impacts and excessive atmospheric speed can destroy the ship.
  Lower gear and approach below 8 m/s relative to the port to dock.
  Orbital stations move and rotate. Match their velocity, request D
  clearance, then enter the central opening through the marked corridor.
  Autopilot requests clearance and waits outside the corridor if busy.

@endnode

@node navigation "Navigation and Time"
@toc main
@prev flight
@next contracts

  [ / ] decrease/increase time: 1, 4, 16, 64, 256, 1024, 4096x.
  High rates require autopilot and clear space; hazards reduce the rate.

  Y opens the system destination list. T selects inhabited ports;
  Q selects body waypoints. Up/Down changes the selected list's target.
  Z opens live top-down orbits. C toggles system/local scale around the
  selected body, or its parent for moons. Q/T and Up/Down select destinations.
  Local scale resolves moons and stations. White stars, green settled bodies,
  cyan other bodies, gold target and red ship use true-scale projected orbits.
  Small bodies and ports can overlap at system scale.
  I shows economy, government, body radius/gravity/atmosphere, rings, parent,
  orbit, period, inclination, ports, survey status and navigation range.
  Y returns to the list. V returns to flight, then P engages its autopilot.
  Body waypoints stop above the atmosphere. Gas giants cannot be landed on.
  Autopilot follows elevated waypoints around planets and central stars.

  Planets have realistic radii and astronomical orbital separations.
  Stars, planets, moons and stations move in analytic circular orbits.
  Flight velocities are relative to the current translated local frame.
  Journeys may take hours or days of simulated time. Fuel use follows
  engine impulse; small cruise corrections cost less than full thrust.

  ] raises requested time compression; [ reduces it.
  The HUD shows effective rate / requested rate.
  Guidance lowers the effective rate near terrain, traffic, the
  destination and braking limits. Manual flight stays at normal speed.
  Compression uses repeated 1/32-second physics ticks.

  G opens the galaxy map. Arrows select a sector. C centres on the
  current system. X jumps when above 12 km with sufficient fuel,
  no nearby traffic and a destination within six sectors.
  J shows contract destination sectors and port numbers.
  Empty galaxy cells reject jumps. Star density follows four spiral arms.
  Unsettled systems have no ports; use Q and P to explore their bodies.
  Green map markers show previously visited systems. The commander
  journal records up to 128 systems and their visited/surveyed bodies.
  An approach below 100 km marks a body surveyed.

@endnode

@node contracts "Port Contracts"
@toc main
@prev navigation
@next trade

  Dock, press B, select an offer with 1-8, then Return to accept.
  J lists contracts. Deadlines are in simulated time.

  Courier / Freight: dock at the destination sector and port.
  Freight reserves cargo space until the contract ends.

  Orbit Patrol / Bounty: a hostile appears above 13 km altitude.
  Air Patrol: a hostile appears between 600 and 6000 m.
  Disable the target with the laser to complete the combat contract.

  Survey: fly between 500 and 6000 m below 200 m/s for twenty seconds.

  Payment is automatic and occurs once. Expired contracts fail.
  These are simple prototype templates, not the complete career system.

@endnode

@node trade "Trading and Ship Services"
@toc main
@prev contracts
@next saving

  M opens the market. Select a commodity with 1-8.
  + buys one tonne; - sells one tonne. Transactions require docking.
  Your hold includes space reserved by active freight contracts.
  The screen shows separate buy and sell prices for each commodity.
  Each port keeps its own supply. Purchases raise scarcity prices;
  selling increases stock and lowers prices. Supply recovers towards
  its generated level by one tonne per simulated hour, even while away.
  Saves preserve up to 128 traded ports across jumps. A full ledger
  refuses trades at new ports; existing markets remain usable.

  U opens the shipyard.
  F refuels and repairs for 100 credits.
  O buys a laser upgrade for 2000 credits, up to three upgrades.
  N buys a larger hull; the displayed cost depends on your ship.
  L launches again.

  Cinder, Loom and Hearth hulls have 20, 60 and 140 tonne holds.
  The full equipment catalogue and civilian economy are unfinished.

@endnode

@node saving "Commander Saves"
@toc main
@prev trade
@next hardware

  F5 saves to PROGDIR:space.sav. F9 loads the commander.
  Native modes restore the OS for file access, then resume takeover.
  The save includes its format/generator version and checksum, exploration
  journal, local frame, accumulated fuel impulse, docking approach and markets.
  Save format 5 keeps world generator version 3. Version 3 commanders load
  with saved supply assigned to the docked port; other ports use generated
  supply. Old version 2 prototype commanders remain incompatible.
  A rejected or corrupt load leaves the current game unchanged.

  New data is written and closed before replacing the old slot.
  On filesystems that cannot replace via Rename, the old slot is kept
  as space.sav.bak. A failed replacement attempts to restore it.
  Version 4 commanders also load. Old open-ground landings are attached to
  the rotating terrain; docked commanders follow their moving port.
  Prototype save formats can change between development revisions.

@endnode

@node hardware "Hardware Backends"
@toc main
@prev saving
@next limits

  ECS uses PAL 320 x 256 or NTSC 320 x 200, five planes, 32 colours.
  --lowres selects centred 320 x 200 on PAL, reducing pixel work.
  At 200 lines, menus use the whole screen to keep all rows visible.
  Native timing uses 50 Hz PAL or 60 Hz NTSC with fixed physics ticks.
  AGA uses eight planes, 256 palette entries with 24-bit precision,
  64-bit display fetches and extra terrain/atmosphere colour ranges.
  Native output owns its Copper lists and double-buffered Chip RAM.
  OS scheduling and interrupts are disabled during native play.

  RTG uses a 640 x 480 driver-managed display and safe buffer messages.
  Its OS remains running because the graphics-card driver requires it.
  This is the portable RTG path; direct registers differ between cards.

  Simulation uses integer coordinates, a lookup trig table and fixed
  physics ticks. The 68000 and 68020 executables require no FPU.

@endnode

@node limits "Current Prototype Limits"
@toc main
@prev hardware
@next help

  Orbits are inclined circles, not a full gravitational N-body solver.
  Gravity follows the nearest body and adds solar gravity in space.
  Terrain LOD edges share vertices and use a skirt to the coarse globe.
  Surfaces have seeded rock strata, mineral deposits, desert dune ridges,
  ice fractures, vegetation clearings and lunar crater/ejecta patterns.
  Detail stays anchored to the rotating planet and fades with distance.
  These are visual textures; terrain heights and landing pads are unchanged.

  Planets rotate about their Z axes. Terrain, forests and surface ports
  follow that rotation; port velocities include ground motion. Moons are
  landable and gas giants can have multiple satellites and equatorial rings.
  Stellar geometry controls day/night and planet/moon eclipses. Atmosphere
  colours approximate Rayleigh single scattering above the solar horizon.
  Temperate/frozen weather has a permeable curved cloud deck, cockpit fog,
  rain and snow. Flora grows on temperate land, with polar ice and dry rock.
  Clouds and precipitation are bounded procedural visual models, without
  fluid dynamics, accumulation or hydrology. Trees/buildings are decorative.
  The renderer uses a depth buffer. Docking walls have swept collisions.
  Clearance traffic is scheduled; it is not a persistent civilian fleet.

  Mining, passengers, crew, factions, military careers, richer missions,
  police, missiles and countermeasures remain to be built.
  Analogue/CD32 input and RTG high-colour are implemented; physical QA remains.
  NTSC ECS output has emulator evidence; physical PAL/NTSC QA remains.
  Real-hardware frame rates have not been established.

  Passing specific regression tests does not prove absence of all bugs.
  See the source repository's docs/roadmap.md for the full development list.

@endnode

@node help "Quick Help"
@toc main
@prev limits

  Launch L / Port T / Body Q / Auto P / Clearance D / Flight V
  System ports Y / Galaxy G / Jump X / Contract log J
  Contracts B / Market M / Shipyard U
  Steer arrows / Set speed W S / Fire Ctrl / Assist E / Gear K
  Time [ ] / Pause Space / Save F5 / Load F9 / Exit Escape

  @{"Flight details" link flight}
  @{"Navigation    " link navigation}
  @{"Contents      " link main}

  @{b}Sound and expanded controllers@{ub}
  Original PCM music and engine, laser, damage, explosion, docking and jump
  effects use Paula DMA in native modes and audio.device stereo in RTG.
  --nosound disables audio. Saves restart native music; pause mutes it.
  No external sound assets or ROMs are bundled.

  CD32 (lowlevel.library V40+): red fire, blue brake, yellow radar contact,
  green autopilot, forward/reverse thrust and Play pause.
  --analog selects a physical POT-counter joystick in port 2. Keep it
  centred at startup; press C to recalibrate. Physical devices need QA.

  @{b}Release and timing@{ub}
  CPU builds: Space.000/.020/.030/.040/.060, all without an FPU requirement.
  --rtg16 opens high-colour output through the CGX/P96 driver. The renderer
  still uses its original indexed scene palette. --rtg retains 8-bit output.
  --profile exits after 120 frames and reports render/output timings.
  Space-benchmark.000/.020 measures scene rendering and C2P into Chip RAM.

  Open Install with SYS:Utilities/Installer V42.12+ and choose a drawer.
  Existing space.sav is preserved. CPU icons accept MODE=ECS/AGA/RTG/RTG16
  and LOWRES, NOSOUND, ANALOG tooltypes. Play-AGA/Play-RTG/Play-RTG16 scripts
  use Space.020; Play-ECS-Lowres uses Space.000.
  The original boot ADF starts ECS 320 x 200 with a user-supplied OS3 ROM.
  Tested with 2 MiB Chip and 8 MiB Fast; unexpanded A500s are not validated.
  Escape returns to the boot CLI. A writable ADF can hold a commander save.

@endnode
